{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Learning Experience Design and Software Technology", "title": "Interaction Patterns for Learning Software", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting interaction patterns for learning software to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology", "ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "02", "section": "03", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/02/02"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_calibration"]}, "content": "# Interaction Patterns for Learning Software\n\n![Interaction Patterns for Learning Software textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/02-03-interaction-patterns-for-learning-software.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAn interaction pattern is a reusable solution to a recurring interaction problem: the tabbed comparison, the drag-to-sort, the branching scenario, the spaced-retrieval flashcard, the guided hotspot. Experienced designers do not invent interactions from scratch for every screen; they recognize the problem type and reach for a pattern that has worked before, the way a programmer reaches for a known algorithm. This section is about choosing patterns deliberately, because the wrong pattern can defeat a perfectly good learning goal while looking impressive.\n\nThe trap this section disarms is novelty bias: the instinct that a richer, more immersive, more modern interaction must be a better one. A rotatable 3D model feels more advanced than two static tabs, but if the goal is comparison, the 3D interaction can spend the learner's attention on manipulating the object instead of comparing objects. The right question is never \"which interaction is most engaging?\" but \"which pattern's structure mirrors the cognitive operation the learner must perform?\"\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer shows a stakeholder two prototypes of the same chemistry lesson. One uses a slick, rotatable 3D molecule the learner can spin with a finger; the other uses two plain tabs and a side-by-side overlay. The stakeholder's instinct is immediate: \"The 3D one is obviously better, it feels like real software.\" Then the pilot data arrives. Learners on the 3D version spent forty percent of their time rotating models and only one in six actually placed the molecules side by side to compare them, which was the entire point of the lesson. The impressive interaction had crowded out the cognitive operation it was supposed to support.\n\nThe case shows why interaction choices cannot be settled by how the screen feels in a demo. The stakeholder's \"obviously better\" was a judgment about production value, not about learning. The storyboard's job is to record which cognitive operation the lesson requires (here, comparison) and to justify the chosen pattern against that operation, so that \"it feels modern\" never gets to win the argument by default.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe central principle is **interaction-cognition fit**: the structure of an interaction pattern should mirror the cognitive operation the learner must perform. If the operation is *compare*, the pattern should hold both items in view at once and make differences salient (a side-by-side or overlay), because comparison requires simultaneous access. If the operation is *sequence*, a drag-to-order pattern externalizes the ordering the learner is building. If the operation is *decide under consequences*, a branching scenario lets a choice produce a downstream result. When the pattern's structure matches the operation, the interface disappears and the learner's effort lands on the content. When it mismatches, the interface itself becomes a task.\n\nThree failure modes recur, and each maps to an anchor:\n\n- **Split attention** (from cognitive load theory): the pattern forces the learner to hold information from one place while acting in another, as when a 3D model must be rotated to a remembered orientation before it can be compared. The fix is spatial contiguity, putting the to-be-related elements together.\n- **Seductive detail** (from Mayer's coherence principle): an interaction adds motion, sound, or interactivity that is engaging but irrelevant to the goal, drawing attention away from the operation. Rotation that is not needed for comparison is seductive detail.\n- **Interaction cost asymmetry** (from UDL learner variability): a pattern that is trivial for one learner (precise mouse dragging) is a barrier for another (motor impairment, touchscreen, screen reader). The fix is choosing patterns with low and equitable interaction cost, or offering an equivalent path.\n\nUse these as a triage. Before adopting a pattern because it looks good, ask which of the three failure modes it risks, and whether the cognitive operation actually needs the richness you are about to add.\n\nInteraction-cognition fit means the pattern's structure mirrors the operation. Match each cognitive operation to the pattern whose structure enacts it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Compare two items\",\"definition\":\"Side-by-side or overlay pattern, because comparison needs simultaneous access to both items at once\"},{\"term\":\"Sequence steps in order\",\"definition\":\"Drag-to-order pattern, because it externalizes the ordering the learner is building\"},{\"term\":\"Decide under consequences\",\"definition\":\"Branching scenario, because a choice must produce a downstream result the learner can see\"},{\"term\":\"Observe a process unfold\",\"definition\":\"Learner-paced animation, because the operation is tracing change over time\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A lesson goal is 'compare two molecular shapes.' A designer picks a rotatable 3D model because it 'feels modern.' Why is this a likely interaction-cognition mismatch?\" explanation=\"A 3D model enacts manipulate the object, but comparison needs simultaneous access to both items. Rotating to a remembered orientation imposes split attention, and the rotation itself is seductive detail for a comparison goal. The structure of the pattern does not mirror the operation the goal requires.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is not a mismatch; richer interactions always help comparison\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The pattern enacts manipulation, not simultaneous comparison, so attention goes to the interface instead of the contrast\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The only problem is that 3D is harder to build\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Choosing an Interaction Pattern\n\nHere is a before-and-after decision about one interaction in a chemistry module whose goal is \"compare two molecular shapes and predict which bonds more readily.\"\n\n**Before (pattern v1)**\n\nThe frame used an animated, rotatable 3D model of each molecule. The designer note read: \"3D feels immersive and modern.\"\n\n**The decision point.** Pilot eye-tracking on 6 learners showed:\n\n1. Learners spent about 40 percent of their time rotating the models.\n2. Only 1 of 6 placed the two molecules side by side to compare them.\n3. The closing prediction question was answered correctly by 2 of 6.\n\nThe interaction produced motion and engagement but not the comparison the learning goal required.\n\n**After (pattern v2)**\n\nThe 3D model was replaced with a familiar tabbed comparison: two tabs, each showing a labeled static shape, plus a third tab holding a side-by-side overlay. A drop-to-rank step then asks learners to place \"bonds more readily\" against \"bonds less readily,\" and the drop closes the loop by revealing a one-line rationale.\n\nDesigner note (decision level): \"I cut the 3D rotation because the **signaling** and split-attention costs pulled effort away from comparison. The tabbed pattern is familiar, so attention goes to the molecules, not the interface, and the ranking step gives the interaction a visible consequence.\"\n\nThe revision is defensible because the new pattern is justified by evidence (eye-tracking, the 2-of-6 result), the cognitive demand of the task (comparison), and a closed loop (the ranking reveals a rationale), not by which pattern looked more impressive.\n\nDiagnose the v1-to-v2 switch one move at a time, predicting each before the reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Diagnosing the pattern mismatch\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Eye-tracking shows learners spend 40 percent of time rotating and only 1 of 6 places the molecules side by side. What does this measure about the pattern?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"That the chemistry content is too hard\",\"That the pattern enacts manipulation while the goal requires comparison, so attention lands on the interface\",\"That 3D is simply too slow to render\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The behavior localizes the fault to interaction-cognition fit: the pattern enacts the wrong operation, so the low score reflects the mismatch, not the difficulty of chemistry.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The replacement is a familiar tabbed side-by-side with an overlay tab. Why does familiarity help here rather than seeming dull?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Familiarity is always preferable to novelty\",\"Because a familiar interface costs almost no attention, so effort goes to the molecules, not to learning the controls\",\"Because tabs render faster than 3D\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Novelty in the interface is a cost the learner pays in attention. A familiar pattern frees that attention for the comparison, which is exactly the germane work the goal requires.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The added drop-to-rank step closes the loop: ranking bonds-more-readily against bonds-less-readily forces the comparison to produce a commitment, and revealing a one-line rationale gives the action a visible consequence rather than a bare click.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nName the cognitive operation first, then pick the pattern whose structure matches it, then add richness only if the operation requires it. Richness for its own sake is a liability, not a feature.\n\nBefore you lock an interaction pattern, run it through these tests:\n\n1. **Operation fit:** What cognitive operation must the learner perform, and does the pattern's structure mirror it (compare needs simultaneity, sequence needs ordering, decide needs consequence)?\n2. **Failure-mode scan:** Does the pattern risk split attention, seductive detail, or interaction-cost asymmetry?\n3. **Closed loop:** Does the learner's action produce a visible consequence (a revealed rationale, a downstream branch), or does it just register a click?\n4. **Equity:** Can a keyboard or screen-reader learner perform the same operation at comparable cost?\n\nA pattern that passes is justified by the operation it serves. A pattern that fails the first test is decoration, however polished it looks in a demo.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading is that interactivity equals learning, so adding more interactions makes a screen more educational. Interactivity helps only when each interaction enacts the cognitive operation the goal requires; an interaction that enacts something else (rotating when the goal is comparing) competes with learning rather than supporting it. Count operation-relevant interactions, not interactions.\n\nA second misreading is that a familiar pattern is a boring one to be avoided. In the worked example, the tabbed comparison \"wins\" precisely because it is familiar: the learner's attention does not have to be spent learning the interface, so it can go to the molecules. Novelty in the interaction is a cost the learner pays in attention. Spend that cost only when the operation genuinely needs a pattern the learner has not seen.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nReplay the worked example as a diagnosis. The eye-tracking data is the symptom: forty percent of time on rotation, one of six placing molecules side by side, two of six answering correctly. The designer reads those numbers through interaction-cognition fit and reaches a precise diagnosis: the 3D pattern enacts *manipulate the object*, but the goal requires *compare two objects*, so the pattern and the operation are mismatched. The mismatch, not the difficulty of chemistry, explains the low scores.\n\nWatch how the replacement pattern is engineered to fit. The tabbed comparison with a side-by-side overlay gives simultaneous access, which is what comparison needs, and being familiar it costs the learner almost no attention to operate. Then the drop-to-rank step is added so the interaction has a consequence: ranking \"bonds more readily\" against \"bonds less readily\" forces the comparison to produce a commitment, and revealing a one-line rationale closes the loop. Each design move is traceable to a named principle (spatial contiguity, low interaction cost, closed feedback), not to which prototype looked more impressive.\n\nThe lesson of the walkthrough is that interaction quality is measurable behaviorally. You do not have to argue about whether 3D is \"better\"; you watch where attention goes and whether the operation happens. A designer who collects that evidence can defend a plain pattern over a flashy one to any stakeholder.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, build a small catalog: for each interaction pattern you meet, write the cognitive operation it best serves and the failure mode it most easily slips into. Tabbed comparison serves *compare* and rarely fails; carousel serves *browse* but risks hiding items learners need to compare; auto-playing animation serves *observe a process* but courts seductive detail. Over time this catalog becomes the professional judgment that lets you pick a pattern in seconds and defend it.\n\nA strong reader of interaction patterns can answer four questions about any learning interaction:\n\n1. What cognitive operation does this pattern's structure actually enact?\n2. Does that match the operation the learning goal requires?\n3. Which of the three failure modes (split attention, seductive detail, cost asymmetry) is this pattern most likely to trigger?\n4. What behavioral evidence (time-on-task, completion of the operation, transfer) would confirm the pattern is working?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong artifact for this section names the cognitive operation explicitly in the storyboard and justifies the chosen pattern against it, ideally with at least one rejected pattern and the failure mode that ruled it out. The strongest versions cite behavioral evidence (real or projected) for why the chosen pattern lets the operation actually happen, rather than asserting that it \"engages\" learners.\n\nWhen you consult BigAL, ask it to argue the opposite pattern. A productive prompt is: \"My goal requires the learner to compare two items, and I chose a tabbed side-by-side pattern. Make the strongest case for an alternative pattern, and tell me what learner behavior would prove my choice wrong.\" Forcing the tool to defend the alternative surfaces failure modes you are too close to see.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nKeep the cognitive operation (compare two molecular shapes) but change the goal from \"predict which bonds more readily\" to \"explain step by step why one bonds more readily.\" The operation has shifted from compare to compare-then-explain-a-process, and that should change the pattern. The static side-by-side that served pure comparison may now be too still, because the new goal requires the learner to trace a sequence of interactions over time. Here a controlled, learner-paced animation with the two structures contiguous might earn the richness that the original goal would have rejected as seductive detail. The same content and the same pair of molecules, read against a different operation, can flip your earlier verdict on animation. If you can explain why animation is seductive detail for \"predict\" but germane for \"explain the process,\" you understand interaction-cognition fit rather than a fixed list of good and bad patterns.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain in your own words why the same controlled animation can be seductive detail for one goal and germane processing for another, using the predict-versus-explain-the-process pair.\" answer=\"Whether motion helps depends on the cognitive operation, not on the medium. For 'predict which bonds more readily,' the operation is comparison, which needs simultaneous static access; motion adds nothing the operation requires and draws attention away, so it is seductive detail. For 'explain step by step why one bonds more readily,' the operation is tracing a process over time, which motion directly enacts, so the same animation now carries the germane work. The element is identical; its classification flips with the operation it must serve.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/02-03-interaction-patterns-for-learning-software-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"02.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 02.03. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Interaction Patterns for Learning Software, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software", "stem": "A module replaces three static text screens with three flip-card animations containing the same words. What is the most likely effect on learning?", "options": ["Learning improves because the content is now interactive.", "Learning is unlikely to improve, because the flips add motion but require no new thinking from the learner.", "Learning improves because animation always increases engagement.", "Learning declines only if the cards load slowly."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Interaction is instructional only when it drives generative processing. Flipping a card to reveal identical text adds motion, not cognitive work.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_clicks_equal_learning", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software", "stem": "For categorizing 8 examples into 3 groups, a designer invents a custom two-finger swipe gesture instead of using a familiar tabbed sort. When is the custom gesture justified?", "options": ["Always, because custom interactions make the module memorable.", "Only when the familiar tabbed pattern genuinely fails the task, since a familiar pattern lets learners spend attention on content rather than on learning the interface.", "Never; instructional software must avoid all gestures.", "Whenever the design team finds tabs visually boring."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Familiar interaction patterns reduce extraneous load by reusing prior interface knowledge. Reserve novel patterns for cases where the familiar one cannot do the job.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_novelty_pattern", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software", "stem": "A drag-and-drop step lets learners place molecules into bonding slots, but after dropping, the screen never changes and no hint appears. What is missing from the interaction pattern?", "options": ["A scoreboard showing the class ranking.", "A visible consequence: the loop never closes because the learner sees no result, hint, or change after acting.", "A second drag-and-drop step on the same screen.", "Background music to signal completion."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Every learning interaction needs a visible consequence so the loop closes. Input without response is a decorative pattern, not an instructional one.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_no_feedback_in_loop", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "You must defend choosing a simple tabbed comparison over an animated 3D model for showing two protein shapes. Which justification is the strongest evidence-based revision note?", "options": ["The tabs render faster than the 3D model.", "Pilot eye-tracking showed learners spent 40 percent of time rotating the 3D model and never compared the shapes; the tabs put both shapes side by side so attention went to the comparison the task requires.", "The 3D model looked unprofessional.", "Tabs are the most common pattern on the web."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The defensible note ties the pattern choice to evidence (eye-tracking) and to the cognitive demand of the task (comparison), not to speed or appearance.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_clicks_equal_learning", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_clicks_equal_learning", "pattern": "interactivity_mistaken_for_learning", "description": "Assuming that more clicks, drags, or animations mean more learning regardless of what cognitive work they require", "trigger": "learner adds hotspots, carousels, or flip cards without specifying the thinking each interaction provokes", "feedback": "Interaction is instructional only when it drives generative processing. For each pattern, name the thinking it requires, not the motion it produces.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_novelty_pattern", "pattern": "novel_pattern_over_familiar_pattern", "description": "Choosing an unusual interaction pattern for distinctiveness even when a familiar pattern would lower extraneous load", "trigger": "learner picks a custom gesture or bespoke widget where a standard accordion or tab would let learners reuse prior interface knowledge", "feedback": "A familiar interaction pattern lets learners spend attention on content, not on the interface. Reserve novel patterns for when the familiar one truly fails the task.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_3_interaction_patterns_for_learning_software_no_feedback_in_loop", "pattern": "interaction_without_consequence", "description": "Designing an interaction that accepts learner input but never responds to it, so the loop never closes", "trigger": "learner builds a drag-and-drop or selection step that shows no result, hint, or change after the action", "feedback": "Every learning interaction needs a visible consequence. Specify what the learner sees or can do differently after acting, or the pattern is decorative.", "rail_action": "represent"}]}